from full_a import C
LONG = {}

LONG["joseph-egypt"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Joseph was", """Joseph was the eleventh of Jacob’s twelve sons and the first son of Jacob’s beloved wife Rachel. He lived about 1900 BC. His family were shepherds living in Canaan, in tents, with their flocks. Jacob’s household was divided by favoritism and jealousy. Jacob had four wives, including Leah, Rachel and their servants, and twelve sons from them.

Joseph’s life takes him from the bottom of a pit to the second-highest position in the greatest empire in the world. It is one of the longest and best-told stories in Genesis.""", ("Genesis", 37)),
("Why his story matters", """Joseph’s story answers a question every person asks: where is God when bad things happen? It shows how God can bring good out of betrayal, injustice and years of waiting.""")],
cards=[
C("The favorite son", "Jacob gave Joseph a special robe and Joseph told his family dreams about ruling over them. His brothers hated him.",
"""Joseph was seventeen. He helped his brothers tend the flocks and brought his father bad reports about them. Jacob loved him more than the others and made him a special robe, often called a “coat of many colors” or a long robe with sleeves, a sign of rank. “And when his brothers saw that their father loved him more than all his brothers, they hated him and could not speak peaceably to him.”

Then Joseph told them two dreams. In one, the brothers’ sheaves of grain bowed down to his. In the other, the sun, moon and eleven stars bowed to him. His brothers said, “Are you indeed to reign over us?” His father rebuked him but kept the matter in mind. Joseph probably did not realize how this would sound. But these dreams were from God.""", ("Genesis", 37)),
C("Sold by his brothers", "His brothers threw him into a pit and sold him to traders heading for Egypt.",
"""Jacob sent Joseph to check on his brothers, who were pasturing the flock near Dothan. When they saw him coming in the distance, they said, “Here comes this dreamer. Come now, let us kill him and throw him into one of the pits.” The oldest, Reuben, persuaded them not to kill him, planning to rescue him later. They stripped off his robe and threw him into an empty cistern.

While they ate, a caravan of traders came by on its way to Egypt. Judah suggested they sell him rather than kill him. They sold Joseph for twenty shekels of silver. They dipped his robe in goat’s blood and brought it to Jacob. “Is it your son’s robe or not?” “It is my son’s robe. A fierce animal has devoured him.” Jacob tore his clothes and mourned for many days. “All his sons and all his daughters rose up to comfort him, but he refused to be comforted.”""", ("Genesis", 37)),
C("Slave, then prisoner", "In Egypt, Joseph rose in Potiphar’s house, was falsely accused and thrown into prison.",
"""In Egypt, Joseph was sold to Potiphar, an officer of Pharaoh and captain of the guard. “The Lord was with Joseph, and he became a successful man.” Potiphar put him in charge of the whole house.

Potiphar’s wife noticed him and wanted him to sleep with her. Joseph refused. “How then could I do this great wickedness and sin against God?” She kept asking, day after day. One day she grabbed his cloak. He ran out, leaving it in her hand. She accused him of attacking her, and Potiphar had him thrown into the king’s prison.

Even there, “the Lord was with Joseph and showed him steadfast love.” The prison warden put him in charge. Two of Pharaoh’s officials, the cupbearer and the baker, were imprisoned and each had a dream. Joseph explained that the cupbearer would be restored in three days, and the baker would be executed. Joseph asked the cupbearer, “Remember me, when it is well with you.” Both things happened as Joseph said. “Yet the chief cupbearer did not remember Joseph, but forgot him.” Two more years passed.""", ("Genesis", 39)),
C("Pharaoh’s dream", "Pharaoh dreamed of seven fat cows eaten by seven thin cows. Joseph explained the dream and was made ruler of Egypt.",
"""Pharaoh had two troubling dreams. In one, seven fat cows came up out of the Nile, followed by seven thin cows that ate them. In the other, seven good ears of grain were swallowed by seven thin ears. No one could explain. The cupbearer finally remembered Joseph. Joseph was brought out, shaved and changed his clothes. When Pharaoh said he heard Joseph could interpret dreams, he answered, “It is not in me; God will give Pharaoh a favorable answer.”

The dreams meant that Egypt would have seven years of great harvests, followed by seven years of terrible famine. Joseph also suggested a plan: store up grain during the good years. Pharaoh said, “Can we find a man like this, in whom is the Spirit of God?” He made Joseph, who was thirty, the second most powerful man in Egypt. He gave him his signet ring, a gold chain, fine clothing and an Egyptian wife, Asenath. They had two sons, Manasseh and Ephraim. “For God has made me forget all my hardship... God has made me fruitful in the land of my affliction.”""", ("Genesis", 41)),
C("His brothers return", "The famine reached Canaan, and Joseph’s brothers came to Egypt to buy grain. They did not recognize him.",
"""The famine spread, and Jacob sent ten of his sons to Egypt to buy grain. Joseph recognized them at once, but they bowed to him without knowing who he was, just as in his dreams. He spoke harshly to them, accused them of being spies and put them in prison for three days. He kept Simeon as a hostage and told the rest to bring their youngest brother, Benjamin.

Joseph was testing them to see if they had changed. When they returned with Benjamin, he had his silver cup hidden in Benjamin’s sack and then accused him of theft. He said that the one who had the cup would become his slave. Judah, who had once suggested selling Joseph, stepped forward: “Let your servant remain instead of the boy as a servant to my lord, and let the boy go back with his brothers. For how can I go back to my father if the boy is not with me?” It was the evidence Joseph needed.""", ("Genesis", 44)),
C("“God meant it for good”", "Joseph revealed himself and forgave his brothers, telling them that God had sent him ahead to save lives.",
"""Joseph could not hold back any longer. He sent everyone else out of the room and wept so loudly that the Egyptians heard it. “I am Joseph! Is my father still alive?” His brothers were too shocked to answer. “Come near to me, please... I am your brother, Joseph, whom you sold into Egypt. And now do not be distressed or angry with yourselves because you sold me here, for God sent me before you to preserve life.”

Joseph invited his whole family to move to Egypt. Jacob, now 130, saw his son again after twenty-two years. The family settled in Goshen. After Jacob died, the brothers were afraid Joseph would take revenge. Joseph wept and said, “Do not fear, for am I in the place of God? As for you, you meant evil against me, but God meant it for good, to bring it about that many people should be kept alive, as they are today.” He lived to 110 and, near the end, made the Israelites promise to carry his bones back to the promised land when God brought them out. Centuries later, they did.""", ("Genesis", 50))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Joseph spent thirteen years as a slave and prisoner for something he did not do. The Bible repeats, “the Lord was with Joseph.” It does not say God removed the trouble, but He was there in it, and He was working toward something Joseph could not yet see.

His forgiveness is also remarkable. He had every reason for revenge and the power to carry it out. Instead he said, “You meant evil against me, but God meant it for good.” He did not call what they had done good. He said God was able to use it.

If you are in a pit or a prison right now, Joseph’s story is a reminder that the story is not over. God is able to bring good out of even the worst things, and He keeps His promises. In the end, Joseph’s bones went home.""", ("Genesis", 50))])

LONG["mary-magdalene"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Mary Magdalene was", """Mary Magdalene was a follower of Jesus from a town called Magdala, on the west shore of the Sea of Galilee. She is named in the Gospels more than most of the other women who traveled with Jesus. She is also named in every list of the women at the cross and the tomb, always near the front.

The Bible says that Jesus had driven seven demons out of her. Luke says she and other women supported Jesus and the twelve “out of their means.” That suggests she had some money and was willing to give it to Jesus’ work.""", ("Luke", 8)),
("What the Bible does not say", """Many people think Mary Magdalene was a prostitute. The Bible never says that. That idea came from a sermon by Pope Gregory in the sixth century, which mixed her up with an unnamed sinful woman in another story. The Bible’s own picture of her is a woman who was set free from a terrible oppression and followed Jesus faithfully, even to the cross and the tomb.""")],
cards=[
C("Set free", "Jesus freed Mary from seven demons, and she followed Him from then on.",
"""Luke writes that Jesus went through cities and villages, proclaiming the good news of the kingdom of God. “And the twelve were with him, and also some women who had been healed of evil spirits and infirmities: Mary, called Magdalene, from whom seven demons had gone out, and Joanna, the wife of Chuza, Herod’s household manager, and Susanna, and many others, who provided for them out of their means.”

The Bible does not describe how Jesus freed her. But it says seven demons, a number that suggests severe suffering. Whatever it was, her life was changed. From that point, she was part of Jesus’ circle, traveling with Him and the twelve, helping to support the work. She was one of several women, from different backgrounds, who were among the disciples, a remarkable thing in that time.""", ("Luke", 8)),
C("At the cross", "When the male disciples had run away, Mary Magdalene stood near the cross with Jesus’ mother and other women.",
"""When Jesus was crucified, most of His male disciples were nowhere to be found. But the Gospels say that several women stayed. “Standing by the cross of Jesus were his mother and his mother’s sister, Mary the wife of Clopas, and Mary Magdalene.” They watched from a distance as He died, with other women who had followed from Galilee.

Roman crucifixion was horrible, and spectators could be arrested for being associated with the condemned. They stayed anyway. After He died, Joseph of Arimathea and Nicodemus took His body down and laid it in a tomb. “Mary Magdalene and Mary the mother of Joses saw where he was laid.” The women watched so they would know where to return after the Sabbath to anoint His body properly.""", ("John", 19)),
C("The empty tomb", "Early on Sunday, Mary came to the tomb in the dark, found the stone rolled away, and ran to tell Peter and John.",
"""“Now on the first day of the week Mary Magdalene came to the tomb early, while it was still dark, and saw that the stone had been taken away from the tomb.” She did not wait to see more. She ran to Simon Peter and John and said, “They have taken the Lord out of the tomb, and we do not know where they have laid him.”

Peter and John ran to the tomb. John arrived first, looked in and saw the linen cloths. Peter went in and saw the cloths lying there and the face cloth folded up by itself. John then went in, “and he saw and believed.” The two disciples went home. Mary stayed outside the tomb weeping. She did not know yet what had happened. All she knew was that the body of the Lord she loved was gone.""", ("John", 20)),
C("“Mary”", "Mary saw two angels and then a man she thought was the gardener. When He said her name, she knew it was Jesus.",
"""As she wept, she stooped and looked into the tomb. Two angels in white sat where Jesus’ body had been. “Woman, why are you weeping?” “Because they have taken away my Lord, and I do not know where they have laid him.” Then she turned and saw a man standing there, but did not realize it was Jesus. “Woman, why are you weeping? Whom are you seeking?” Thinking He was the gardener, she said, “Sir, if you have carried him away, tell me where you have laid him, and I will take him away.”

Jesus said, “Mary.” That was all. “She turned and said to him in Aramaic, ‘Rabboni!’ (which means Teacher).” The way He said her name was enough. She had followed Him for years, and now He was alive. She reached out for Him, and Jesus told her, “Do not cling to me, for I have not yet ascended to the Father; but go to my brothers and say to them, ‘I am ascending to my Father and your Father, to my God and your God.’”""", ("John", 20)),
C("The first witness", "Mary went to the disciples and announced, “I have seen the Lord.”",
"""“Mary Magdalene went and announced to the disciples, ‘I have seen the Lord’ — and that he had said these things to her.” She was the first person to see the risen Jesus and the first to tell others. In the first century, a woman’s testimony was not given much weight in a court. Yet God chose to make a woman the first witness of the central event of history.

Mark’s Gospel confirms that Jesus “appeared first to Mary Magdalene, from whom he had cast out seven demons.” Early Christian writers later called her “the apostle to the apostles,” since she was the one sent to tell the apostles. The Gospels say that at first, the men did not believe her story. “These words seemed to them an idle tale, and they did not believe them.” They believed only when Jesus appeared to them Himself.

The Bible does not tell us more about Mary’s later life. But Jesus knew her by name, and she was there at the most important moment in history.""", ("John", 20))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Mary Magdalene’s story is about what Jesus does for a person who is broken. She was a woman who had been tormented and Jesus gave her a new life. Her response was loyal, grateful and costly. Whatever might be tormenting you, the Bible shows that Jesus is stronger than it.

She also teaches us what faithfulness looks like. She stayed at the cross when it was dangerous and went to the tomb when it seemed too late. People who keep showing up, in love, are often the first to see what God is doing.

And the story shows how Jesus knows His people. He did not announce Himself by a title. He said her name, and she knew. Jesus said, “I am the good shepherd... he calls his own sheep by name.” He knows your name, too.""", ("John", 20))])

LONG["elijah"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Elijah was", """Elijah was a prophet in the northern kingdom of Israel, about 870 to 850 BC. He came from Tishbe in Gilead, east of the Jordan, a rugged area. The Bible tells us nothing about his family. He appears suddenly in the story, wearing a rough garment and a leather belt, and goes to the king.

King Ahab, with his wife Jezebel, had led Israel into the worship of Baal. Elijah’s name means “My God is the Lord.” His whole life was an answer to the question of who is God.""", ("1 Kings", 17)),
("Why his story matters", """Elijah is one of the most dramatic people in the Bible. His story has fire from heaven, a miracle at a widow’s house, a ride in a whirlwind, and a time when he sat down under a tree and asked to die. It shows God’s power and God’s tenderness.""")],
cards=[
C("No rain, and the ravens", "Elijah told Ahab there would be no rain until he said so. God sent him to hide by a brook where ravens fed him.",
"""Elijah walked into the court of King Ahab and said, “As the Lord, the God of Israel, lives, before whom I stand, there shall be neither dew nor rain these years, except by my word.” It was a direct challenge to Baal, who was supposed to be the god of rain and storms.

God told him to hide by the brook Cherith, east of the Jordan. “You shall drink from the brook, and I have commanded the ravens to feed you there.” Every morning and evening, ravens brought him bread and meat. After a while the brook dried up because of the drought. God sent him to the town of Zarephath, in the territory of Sidon, which was Jezebel’s own homeland. There a widow was gathering sticks to cook a last meal for herself and her son. Elijah asked her for some bread. “The jar of flour shall not be spent, and the jug of oil shall not be empty, until the day that the Lord sends rain upon the earth.” She trusted him, and for many days the jar and jug did not run out.""", ("1 Kings", 17)),
C("A boy raised", "The widow’s son died. Elijah stretched himself over the boy three times and prayed, and the child came back to life.",
"""Some time later, the widow’s son became sick and died. She said to Elijah, “What have you against me, O man of God? You have come to me to bring my sin to remembrance and to cause the death of my son!” Elijah took the boy from her arms, carried him up to the upper room and laid him on his own bed. He cried out to the Lord, “O Lord my God, let this child’s life return to him.” He stretched himself over the child three times.

“And the Lord listened to the voice of Elijah. And the life of the child returned to him, and he revived.” Elijah gave him back to his mother. She said, “Now I know that you are a man of God, and that the word of the Lord in your mouth is truth.” It is the first account in the Bible of someone being brought back from the dead.""", ("1 Kings", 17)),
C("Showdown on Carmel", "After three years of drought, Elijah challenged 450 prophets of Baal. Fire fell from heaven on his sacrifice.",
"""In the third year, God told Elijah to go to Ahab, and He would send rain. Elijah told Ahab to gather all Israel and the prophets of Baal on Mount Carmel. “How long will you go limping between two different opinions? If the Lord is God, follow him; but if Baal, then follow him.” The people said nothing.

Each side would prepare a bull on an altar. The god who answered by fire would be God. The prophets of Baal called from morning until noon, danced around the altar and cut themselves with swords. Elijah teased them: “Cry aloud, for he is a god. Either he is musing, or he is relieving himself, or he is on a journey, or perhaps he is asleep and must be awakened.” Nothing happened.

At the time of the evening sacrifice, Elijah repaired the Lord’s altar with twelve stones, dug a trench around it and had them pour four large jars of water over the offering three times, until the trench was full. He prayed a brief prayer: “O Lord, God of Abraham, Isaac, and Israel, let it be known this day that you are God in Israel... Answer me, O Lord, answer me, that this people may know that you, O Lord, are God.” Fire fell and burned up the sacrifice, the wood, the stones, the dust and the water. All the people fell on their faces: “The Lord, he is God!” Elijah then prayed seven times for rain, and the servant finally saw a cloud as small as a man’s hand. Then the sky grew black with heavy rain.""", ("1 Kings", 18)),
C("Under the broom tree", "After Jezebel threatened him, Elijah fled into the desert, exhausted and afraid, and asked to die.",
"""Ahab told Jezebel everything. She sent a message: “So may the gods do to me and more also, if I do not make your life as the life of one of them by this time tomorrow.” Elijah, who had just stood against 450 prophets, was afraid. “Then he arose and ran for his life.” He left his servant in Beersheba, went a day’s journey into the wilderness and sat down under a broom tree. “It is enough; now, O Lord, take away my life, for I am no better than my fathers.” He lay down and slept.

An angel touched him: “Arise and eat.” He found a cake baked on hot stones and a jar of water. He ate, drank and slept again. The angel came a second time. “Arise and eat, for the journey is too great for you.” God did not scold him. He fed him and let him rest. Then, strengthened, Elijah walked forty days to Mount Horeb, the mountain where God gave Moses the law.""", ("1 Kings", 19)),
C("The gentle whisper", "At Horeb, God spoke to Elijah not in wind, earthquake or fire, but in a low whisper.",
"""At Horeb, Elijah went into a cave. God asked, “What are you doing here, Elijah?” Elijah answered, “I have been very jealous for the Lord, the God of hosts. For the people of Israel have forsaken your covenant, thrown down your altars and killed your prophets with the sword, and I, even I only, am left, and they seek my life, to take it away.”

God told him to stand on the mountain. “And behold, the Lord passed by, and a great and strong wind tore the mountains and broke in pieces the rocks before the Lord, but the Lord was not in the wind. And after the wind an earthquake, but the Lord was not in the earthquake. And after the earthquake a fire, but the Lord was not in the fire. And after the fire the sound of a low whisper.” Elijah covered his face with his cloak and stood at the mouth of the cave.

Again, God asked what he was doing. Elijah gave the same answer. God gave him new work, to anoint two kings and a successor, Elisha. And He added something Elijah did not know: “Yet I will leave seven thousand in Israel, all the knees that have not bowed to Baal.” Elijah was not alone.""", ("1 Kings", 19)),
C("The whirlwind", "Near the end of his life, Elijah was taken up to heaven in a chariot of fire. He did not die.",
"""Elijah’s last years included a confrontation with Ahab over the murder of Naboth and a message of judgment on the house of Ahab. Then, one day, he and Elisha walked from Gilgal to Bethel, to Jericho and to the Jordan. Elijah struck the water with his cloak, and it divided so they could cross on dry ground. He asked Elisha, “Ask what I shall do for you, before I am taken from you.” “Please let there be a double portion of your spirit on me.”

“And as they still went on and talked, behold, chariots of fire and horses of fire separated the two of them. And Elijah went up by a whirlwind into heaven.” Elisha cried, “My father, my father! The chariots of Israel and its horsemen!” He saw him no more. Only one other person in the Bible, Enoch, is said to have been taken to heaven without dying.

The last verses of the Old Testament promise that God would send Elijah before the great day of the Lord. Jesus said that John the Baptist was the Elijah who was to come. And at the Transfiguration, Elijah appeared with Moses, talking with Jesus.""", ("2 Kings", 2))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Elijah was bold and he was human. On Carmel he stood alone against hundreds, and a few days later he was running in fear. The Bible does not hide that. Spiritual high points are often followed by exhaustion. He was not ashamed to tell God exactly how he felt.

God’s response is very kind. He gave Elijah sleep, food, water and a quiet place. Sometimes what we need most is rest and a meal. After that, He spoke gently, and reminded Elijah that he was not alone.

The whisper at Horeb is also a good lesson. God can use fire and storms, but He often speaks quietly. In a noisy world, we may need to be still to hear Him. Elijah’s story says that God is powerful enough to send fire and gentle enough to be found in a whisper.""", ("1 Kings", 19))])

LONG["martha-mary"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Martha and Mary were", """Martha and Mary were sisters who lived in Bethany, a village near Jerusalem, with their brother Lazarus. Jesus was a close friend of the family and stayed with them when He was in the area. The Gospel of John says, “Jesus loved Martha and her sister and Lazarus.”

The two sisters had very different personalities. Martha was a hard-working organizer and host. Mary was a quiet listener, someone who wanted to be close to Jesus. The Gospels show them three times.""", ("Luke", 10)),
("Why their story matters", """These two sisters show that there is more than one way to love Jesus, and that He values both service and listening. They also show what faith looks like when it is tested by grief.""")],
cards=[
C("A busy house", "Martha welcomed Jesus into her home and worked hard to serve. Mary sat at Jesus’ feet and listened.",
"""Luke tells that Jesus and His disciples were traveling and “a woman named Martha welcomed him into her house.” The Bible says her sister Mary “sat at the Lord’s feet and listened to his teaching.” To sit at a rabbi’s feet was to be his student, a place usually given to men. Jesus welcomed Mary there.

Martha was “distracted with much serving.” There were probably thirteen or more guests to feed. She became frustrated. She came to Jesus and said, “Lord, do you not care that my sister has left me to serve alone? Tell her then to help me.” She was not wrong to want help. She was also criticizing both her sister and the Lord, whom she thought wasn’t paying attention.""", ("Luke", 10)),
C("“One thing is necessary”", "Jesus gently told Martha that she was worried about many things, but Mary had chosen the better part.",
"""Jesus did not scold her. He said her name twice, gently: “Martha, Martha, you are anxious and troubled about many things, but one thing is necessary. Mary has chosen the good portion, which will not be taken away from her.”

Jesus was not saying that serving is bad. Hospitality was important, and Jesus Himself fed people and washed feet. He was pointing out that Martha had let her work push out the one thing that mattered most: being with Him. Her problem was not the work but the worry. And He also defended Mary’s choice to sit and listen, which was an unusual one for a woman of her time. He was telling Martha that He did not need a big meal. He wanted her.""", ("Luke", 10)),
C("Martha’s confession", "When Lazarus died, Martha ran to meet Jesus and declared that He was the Christ, the Son of God.",
"""Some time later, their brother Lazarus fell ill and died. By the time Jesus arrived, he had been in the tomb four days. Martha heard that Jesus was coming and went out to meet Him, while Mary stayed in the house. She said, “Lord, if you had been here, my brother would not have died. But even now I know that whatever you ask from God, God will give you.”

Jesus said, “I am the resurrection and the life. Whoever believes in me, though he die, yet shall he live, and everyone who lives and believes in me shall never die. Do you believe this?” Martha answered with one of the greatest statements of faith in the Gospels: “Yes, Lord; I believe that you are the Christ, the Son of God, who is coming into the world.” That is the same confession Peter made. The practical, busy sister was also a woman of deep faith. Then she went to call Mary quietly: “The Teacher is here and is calling for you.”""", ("John", 11)),
C("Mary at His feet again", "Mary fell at Jesus’ feet in tears. Jesus wept, and then raised Lazarus from the dead.",
"""Mary got up quickly and went to Jesus. She fell at His feet, as she had done before, and said exactly what Martha had said: “Lord, if you had been here, my brother would not have died.” Her tears moved Jesus deeply. “Jesus wept.” He went to the tomb, had the stone taken away and called, “Lazarus, come out!” Lazarus came out, still wrapped in grave clothes.

Both sisters, with different temperaments, had the same grief and the same faith. Each in her own way had brought her pain to Jesus. And both of them saw the glory of God. The same Jesus who said, “One thing is necessary,” gave them back their brother.""", ("John", 11)),
C("Dinner at Bethany", "Six days before Passover, the family gave a dinner for Jesus. Martha served, and Mary poured expensive perfume on His feet.",
"""Six days before Passover, Jesus came back to Bethany. A dinner was held in His honor. “Martha served, and Lazarus was one of those reclining with him at table.” It is worth noticing: Martha is serving again, but there is no complaint this time. She had learned to serve with peace.

Mary took a pound of expensive perfume made of pure nard, poured it on Jesus’ feet and wiped them with her hair. The house was filled with the fragrance. It was worth about a year’s wages. Judas objected: “Why was this ointment not sold for three hundred denarii and given to the poor?” John adds that Judas did not care about the poor but was a thief. Jesus said, “Leave her alone, so that she may keep it for the day of my burial. For the poor you always have with you, but you do not always have me.” Mary seems to have understood what the disciples did not: that Jesus was going to die. Her act of love was remembered. Another Gospel says, “Wherever this gospel is proclaimed in the whole world, what she has done will be told in memory of her.”""", ("John", 12))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """It is easy to see ourselves as Martha, busy with serving and worried about many things. Jesus’ gentle words, “Martha, Martha,” are for everyone who feels too busy to rest at His feet. The Bible is not saying that work does not matter. It is saying that being with Jesus matters most, and that our work flows from it.

But Martha is also a model. She was honest with Jesus. When she was upset, she told Him. When her brother died, she ran to Him. And she gave one of the clearest statements of faith in the Bible.

Mary shows that love is often costly. She poured out something precious without counting the cost. Between them, the sisters show that Jesus welcomes both the doers and the listeners. At the dinner in Bethany, Martha served with joy and Mary worshiped with love, and Jesus was pleased with both.""", ("Luke", 10))])

LONG["noah"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Noah was", """Noah was a descendant of Adam through the line of Seth, born after the world had grown corrupt. His father, Lamech, named him “Noah,” which sounds like the Hebrew for “rest” or “comfort,” saying, “Out of the ground that the Lord has cursed, this one shall bring us relief from our work.”

The Bible says that by the time of Noah, the earth was filled with violence. “The Lord saw that the wickedness of man was great in the earth, and that every intention of the thoughts of his heart was only evil continually.” God was grieved. “But Noah found favor in the eyes of the Lord.”""", ("Genesis", 6)),
("Why his story matters", """Noah’s story is the Bible’s first great story of judgment and rescue. It is also a story of faith that lasted a very long time, and of God’s promise never to destroy the earth by flood again.""")],
cards=[
C("A righteous man", "Noah walked with God in a world full of violence. God told him to build a huge boat.",
"""“Noah was a righteous man, blameless in his generation. Noah walked with God.” The Bible does not say that he was perfect. But he was faithful in a time when almost everyone else was not. He had three sons, Shem, Ham and Japheth.

God told Noah that He was going to destroy all flesh because of the violence. “Make yourself an ark of gopher wood. Make rooms in the ark, and cover it inside and out with pitch.” The ark was to be 300 cubits long, 50 cubits wide and 30 cubits high, about 450 feet by 75 feet by 45 feet, three decks tall, with a door in the side and a window near the top. It was a huge ship, about as long as one and a half football fields.

“Noah did this; he did all that God commanded him.” It was a very big job. The New Testament calls Noah “a herald of righteousness,” which suggests he warned others as he worked. Building it took many years. Imagine the neighbors’ comments about a giant boat on dry land.""", ("Genesis", 6)),
C("Into the ark", "Noah brought animals, his wife, his sons and their wives into the ark, and God shut the door.",
"""God said, “I will establish my covenant with you, and you shall come into the ark, you, your sons, your wife, and your sons’ wives with you.” Noah was to bring two of every kind of animal, male and female, and more of the clean animals, as well as food for everyone. Seven days before the flood began, they all entered. Noah was 600 years old.

“And the Lord shut him in.” That small detail says something: the door was closed by God, not by Noah. The ark was not only a boat but a place of safety. Eight people were inside: Noah, his wife, their three sons and their wives. And the rain began.""", ("Genesis", 7)),
C("The flood", "It rained for forty days and nights. The water covered the highest mountains and lasted about a year.",
"""“On that day all the fountains of the great deep burst forth, and the windows of the heavens were opened. And rain fell upon the earth forty days and forty nights.” The waters rose and lifted the ark above the earth. “The waters prevailed so mightily on the earth that all the high mountains under the whole heaven were covered.” For 150 days the waters held. Every living thing on dry land died.

“But God remembered Noah and all the beasts and all the livestock that were with him in the ark.” God sent a wind over the earth and the waters began to recede. In the seventh month, the ark came to rest on the mountains of Ararat. The tops of the mountains became visible after about two and a half more months.

Noah opened the window and sent out a raven, which flew back and forth. Then he sent out a dove, which returned because it found no place to land. A week later he sent the dove again, and it came back in the evening with a freshly plucked olive leaf in its beak. The third time, the dove did not return. Noah knew the land was drying.""", ("Genesis", 7)),
C("A new start", "After about a year in the ark, God told Noah to come out. Noah built an altar and offered sacrifices.",
"""A year and ten days after the flood began, the earth was dry. God said, “Go out from the ark, you and your wife, and your sons and your sons’ wives with you. Bring out with you every living thing... that they may swarm on the earth, and be fruitful and multiply on the earth.”

The first thing Noah did was build an altar to the Lord. He took some of the clean animals and offered burnt offerings. “And when the Lord smelled the pleasing aroma, the Lord said in his heart, ‘I will never again curse the ground because of man, for the intention of man’s heart is evil from his youth. Neither will I ever again strike down every living creature as I have done.’” Even after the flood, human hearts were still sinful. God did not rescue the world because people had become good. He did it because of His mercy.""", ("Genesis", 8)),
C("The rainbow", "God made a covenant with Noah and set the rainbow in the clouds as a sign.",
"""God blessed Noah and his sons and told them to fill the earth. Then He made a promise: “I establish my covenant with you and your offspring after you... never again shall all flesh be cut off by the waters of the flood, and never again shall there be a flood to destroy the earth.”

“I have set my bow in the cloud, and it shall be a sign of the covenant between me and the earth. When I bring clouds over the earth and the bow is seen in the clouds, I will remember my covenant that is between me and you.” The Hebrew word for rainbow is the same as the word for a warrior’s bow. God hung up His weapon in the sky, pointing away from the earth.

The Bible then tells of one more sad scene. Noah planted a vineyard, got drunk and lay naked in his tent. His son Ham treated him disrespectfully, and the family was affected. Noah lived 350 years after the flood and died at 950. Even the best people are not perfect. The story ends where it began: with the need for someone better.""", ("Genesis", 9))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Noah trusted God when there was no rain, no sign of a flood and nobody else believed. He built for many years based on what God had said. The New Testament says, “By faith Noah, warned by God concerning events as yet unseen, in reverent fear constructed an ark for the saving of his household.” Faith often means acting on God’s word before we see the results.

The ark itself is a picture of salvation. There was one door, one way in. Those who went in were saved, and God Himself shut the door. Jesus said, “I am the door. If anyone enters by me, he will be saved.”

And the rainbow is a daily reminder. Every time one appears in the sky after a storm, it tells us that God keeps His promises. The storm does not have the last word.""", ("Hebrews", 11))])

LONG["david"] = dict(intro=[
("Who David was", """David, son of Jesse from Bethlehem, was Israel’s greatest king, ruling about 1010 to 970 BC. He started as the youngest of eight brothers, a shepherd boy no one thought important. He became a musician, a warrior, a poet, a fugitive and finally a king. The Bible calls him “a man after God’s own heart.”

He also wrote many of the Psalms, the songs and prayers that people have used for 3,000 years. The Gospel writers call Jesus “the Son of David.”""", ("1 Samuel", 16)),
("Why his story matters", """David’s story is long and full. It has victories like his defeat of Goliath and the greatest sin of his life, and shows what a person can do when he is honest with God. It is also the story of a promise God made to David that ends in Jesus.""")],
cards=[
C("The shepherd who was anointed", "Samuel came to Bethlehem looking for the next king, and God chose the youngest son, a shepherd.",
"""God had rejected King Saul, and He sent the prophet Samuel to Jesse’s house in Bethlehem to anoint a new king. Samuel looked at Jesse’s oldest son, tall and impressive, and thought, “Surely the Lord’s anointed is before him.” God said, “Do not look on his appearance or on the height of his stature... For the Lord sees not as man sees: man looks on the outward appearance, but the Lord looks on the heart.”

Seven sons passed by. “Are all your sons here?” Samuel asked. “There remains yet the youngest, but behold, he is keeping the sheep.” David was brought in. “Now he was ruddy and had beautiful eyes and was handsome.” The Lord said, “Arise, anoint him, for this is he.” Samuel poured oil on his head, “and the Spirit of the Lord rushed upon David from that day forward.” For now, David went back to the sheep. He had been told he would be king, but he would not take the throne for about fifteen years.""", ("1 Samuel", 16)),
C("Goliath and Jonathan", "David defeated the giant Goliath. Saul’s son Jonathan became his closest friend.",
"""David first came to the palace to play the harp for King Saul, whose mind was troubled. Then came the famous day in the Valley of Elah, when David, a teenager with a sling and five stones, defeated the Philistine giant Goliath. “You come to me with a sword and with a spear and with a javelin, but I come to you in the name of the Lord of hosts.”

After the battle, David met Saul’s son Jonathan. “The soul of Jonathan was knit to the soul of David, and Jonathan loved him as his own soul.” Jonathan gave him his robe, armor, sword, bow and belt. It was a gift of friendship and also a recognition that David was going to take the throne that would have been Jonathan’s. Jonathan did not resent it. They made a covenant to be loyal to each other and each other’s families.""", ("1 Samuel", 17)),
C("Years on the run", "Saul grew jealous and hunted David for years. Twice David spared Saul’s life.",
"""David was successful in everything Saul sent him to do, and the women sang, “Saul has struck down his thousands, and David his ten thousands.” Saul became jealous, threw a spear at him twice and then hunted him through the wilderness. David lived in caves, gathered about 400 men who were in distress or in debt, and wrote many psalms during these years.

Twice David had a chance to kill Saul. In a cave at En Gedi, Saul came in to relieve himself while David and his men were hiding at the back. David only cut off a corner of his robe, and even that bothered his conscience. “I will not put out my hand against my lord, for he is the Lord’s anointed.” He called after Saul from a distance and showed him the piece of cloth. Saul wept: “You are more righteous than I, for you have repaid me good, whereas I have repaid you evil.” David refused to take the throne by violence. He waited for God to give it.""", ("1 Samuel", 24)),
C("King in Jerusalem", "After Saul died, David became king, first over Judah and then over all Israel. He captured Jerusalem and brought the ark there.",
"""When Saul and Jonathan died in battle, David mourned them with a song. He became king over the tribe of Judah in Hebron at age thirty and reigned there seven and a half years. Then the other tribes came to him, and he became king over all Israel. He captured the fortress of Jebus, which became Jerusalem, “the city of David.”

He brought the ark of the covenant to Jerusalem with shouting and trumpets. “David danced before the Lord with all his might.” Then he told the prophet Nathan he wanted to build a house for God. God answered through Nathan: no, but David’s son would build it. And more: “Your house and your kingdom shall be made sure forever before me. Your throne shall be established forever.” It is a promise that points to Jesus, the greatest Son of David.""", ("2 Samuel", 7)),
C("The great sin", "David took Bathsheba, arranged for her husband Uriah to be killed, and was confronted by the prophet Nathan.",
"""In the spring, when kings went out to battle, David stayed in Jerusalem. One evening from his roof he saw a woman bathing. She was Bathsheba, the wife of Uriah, one of his soldiers. David sent for her and slept with her. She became pregnant. David tried to cover it up by calling Uriah home from the war. When that failed, he sent a letter to Joab, his commander, to put Uriah in the front line of the fiercest fighting and then withdraw. Uriah was killed. David married Bathsheba.

“But the thing that David had done displeased the Lord.” The prophet Nathan told David a story about a rich man who took a poor man’s only lamb. David was furious: “The man who has done this deserves to die!” Nathan said, “You are the man!” David answered, “I have sinned against the Lord.” Nathan said that the Lord had forgiven him, but there would be consequences. The child died, and violence and trouble followed in his family.

David’s prayer of repentance is Psalm 51: “Have mercy on me, O God, according to your steadfast love... Create in me a clean heart, O God, and renew a right spirit within me.”""", ("2 Samuel", 12)),
C("Absalom and the end", "David’s son Absalom rebelled and was killed. David grieved and later prepared the way for Solomon.",
"""David’s family continued to suffer. His son Absalom, handsome and charming, gradually won the loyalty of the people and led a rebellion. David had to flee Jerusalem barefoot and weeping. He told his generals to deal gently with Absalom, but Absalom was caught by his hair in an oak tree and was killed by Joab. When David heard, he cried out, “O my son Absalom, my son, my son Absalom! Would I had died instead of you, O Absalom, my son, my son!”

David returned to Jerusalem. In his last years, he gathered materials for the Temple, which Solomon would build. He charged Solomon: “Be strong, and show yourself a man, and keep the charge of the Lord your God, walking in his ways.” David died at about seventy and was buried in the city of David. His last words in the Bible include: “The Spirit of the Lord speaks by me; his word is on my tongue.”""", ("2 Samuel", 18))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """David was a great man and a great sinner. The Bible shows both honestly. He is called “a man after God’s own heart,” not because he never failed but because when he did, he came back to God. Psalm 51 is the proof. We can come to God with our worst failures and find mercy.

His life also shows how to deal with injustice. When Saul was hunting him, David refused to take matters into his own hands. He trusted God to do what He had promised, in His time.

And the Psalms are David’s gift to everyone. He sang in the cave, in the palace, in joy and in fear. “The Lord is my shepherd; I shall not want.” They are a model for how to pray honestly, and they remind us that the shepherd boy’s greatest descendant, Jesus, is our King forever.""", ("Psalms", 51))])
